The loop condition is `i < items.length - 1`, which stops one index too early.

Arrays are zero-indexed, so the last item sits at `items.length - 1`. With 5 items, the valid indices are 0–4. The condition `i < 4` stops after `i = 3`, so index 4 never gets visited.

Fix it by using either of these:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) { print(items[i]) }
// or
for (let i = 0; i <= items.length - 1; i++) { print(items[i]) }
```

The first is the idiomatic form. If you don't need the index, `for (const item of items) { print(item) }` avoids the problem entirely.

If you skipped the last item on purpose, say to handle it separately (for example, to avoid a trailing comma), the code is fine as written. You'd just need to handle `items[items.length - 1]` after the loop.

